# One "Why this is cited" note per case: the mistake and what should have happened,
# or (for appeals) the correct approach SDC should follow. Keyed by case id.
WHY = {
'stratford-26-01376-FUL':
 "SDC applied the trigger here, and the note uses it as the benchmark. Flood policy F7 is a “should be refused” policy, so the officer treated the failure as engaging S4(2)(c) and refused. The report says the presumption “would not apply”, which goes further than the policy: S4(2)(c) makes the benefits “likely” to be substantially outweighed. The point of section 1 is that a design conflict (DP3(3)) is the same kind of policy and should be treated the same way.",
'stratford-26-00617-PIP':
 "The mistake: the report found “significant harm to the character of the area” in conflict with local design and landscape policies but used DP3(3) only as a source of weight, never asking whether the conflict had clear justification. It also ran the S5(1)(j) test for a Green Belt site before turning to GB7, and declared CS.8 materially inconsistent without saying which part conflicts. What should have happened: if there was no clear justification, the benefits should have been treated as likely to be substantially outweighed (S5(2), which S5(5) applies in the Green Belt), with the report saying whether anything displaced that; and CS.8, whose part B already requires a heritage assessment, keeps its weight except for any part that actually conflicts. The walking-in-the-road point in the first report was a factual correction, not a method error: the Update Report explained that the application proposes a footpath onto Park Lane.",
'stratford-26-01906-PIP':
 "The mistake: the report found harm to the setting of two listed buildings, conflict with HE6 and that the design would be contrary to the 1990 Act, then decided the case on the overall S4 balance alone. What should have happened: the harm should have been weighed against the public benefits as its own step under HE6(4) and HE4(2), given “considerable importance and weight” under HE6(3) and the section 66 duty, and only then carried into the S4 conclusion: the weighing step Ilmington carried out.",
'stratford-26-01399-PIP':
 "This is the heritage method section 2 uses as the benchmark: the officer weighed the harm against the public benefits on its own terms and refused. It is also cited in section 3 because it used the same wording as Snitterfield and Forest Farm to declare CS.8 materially inconsistent, without identifying which part of the policy actually conflicts with the Framework.",
'stratford-26-00922-FUL':
 "The mistake: CS.8 was given “limited weight” because it “does not align” with the new heritage policies, with no explanation of which part conflicts. What should have happened: Annex A(2) reduces weight only for the parts of a policy that are materially inconsistent, so the report should have said which words of CS.8 it meant and kept the rest.",
'stratford-26-00918-PIP':
 "The mistake: the report first applied the S5(1)(j) “well-related to an existing settlement” test to a site in a washed-over Green Belt village, and on location leaned partly on existing use (“Villagers appear to cope”) and on the village's Local Service Village status. What should have happened: a washed-over village is not a “settlement” and S5 does not apply in the Green Belt (S5(5)), so GB6 and GB7 alone, which the report went on to apply, were the right route; and limb (iii) should be decided on the route, the services and the transport for all users, with the Connectivity Tool, which is the test inspectors apply.",
'stratford-26-01458-FUL':
 "The mistake: limb (iii) of GB7(1)(g) was passed because the dwelling sat inside the built-up area boundary of a Local Service Village, with no assessment of the route to services. What should have happened: a built-up area boundary and a village category are policy designations, not evidence of how residents would travel, so the sustainable-location test should have been decided on the real route and TR3.",
'stratford-25-01271-FUL':
 "The mistake: decided on 17 August 2026 under the 2024 Framework and its paragraph 11(d) balance. What should have happened: from the day the 2026 Framework was published its policies had to be taken into account. The site is outside a settlement and not in the Green Belt, so the report should have applied the S5 route: S5(1)(g), traveller and showpeople accommodation meeting an evidenced unmet need, with HO12, or otherwise the S5(4) exceptional-circumstances test.",
'stratford-26-01764-PIP':
 "The mistake: the report ran the 2024 paragraph 11(d) balance and added a note that the outcome was “generally consistent” under both Frameworks. What should have happened: the 2026 Framework replaced 11(d) with policies S3 to S6, so the report should have applied the 2026 test and shown its working rather than carrying the old balance across.",
'stratford-26-01393-FUL':
 "The mistake: like Kineton, the report kept the 2024 balance and added a one-paragraph note that the result was “generally consistent” under both Frameworks. What should have happened: the officer should have applied the 2026 route and test directly, not re-anchored the old reasoning with a closing note.",
'PINS-6011803':
 "This appeal shows the approach on two of the note's points. On a site washed over by the Green Belt, which the parties agreed was not inappropriate, the inspector applied S5(5), not S4: the route section 4 says SDC should take (adding that the result would be the same under S4). And a conflict with a local design standard engaged DP3(3), so “the proposal should be refused”: the trigger Snitterfield cited but used only as weight.",
'PINS-6008785':
 "This appeal shows how a design conflict engages S5(2): the proposal met S5(1)(j), but a conflict with DP3(1) without clear justification engaged DP3(3), and the benefits were “substantially outweighed”. It also treated local policies requiring development to respond to its context as consistent with the Framework, the approach to local policy that section 3 says SDC's CS.8 reasoning leaves out.",
'PINS-6008167':
 "This appeal shows an inspector applying DP3(3) as intended: a conflict with DP3(1) “without clear justification” meant, through S4(2)(c), that the benefits were substantially outweighed. It is the refusal trigger Snitterfield cited but used only as a source of weight.",
'PINS-6005325':
 "This appeal shows the same DP3(3) trigger applied to conflicts with DP3's principles on tree cover and access: the conflict meant the proposal “should be refused” and the housing benefit was substantially outweighed.",
'PINS-6007221':
 "This appeal shows the heritage method section 2 describes: the inspector weighed the harm on its own terms (no clear and convincing justification, considerable importance and weight to the harm) before concluding that the public benefits did not outweigh it, and then carried that into S4. Forest Farm reached its decision without that step.",
'PINS-6008539':
 "This appeal shows that a favourable Green Belt finding does not settle the case: the grey belt finding “does not in itself justify the grant”, and the heritage harm still had to be weighed against the public benefits on its own terms.",
'PINS-6006475':
 "This appeal supports two points. It applied HE4(2) as its own requirement: there was no clear and convincing justification for the harm (section 2). And it treated a local housing policy with heritage and design criteria as consistent with the Framework's broad thrust (section 3).",
'PINS-6007541':
 "This appeal shows local heritage, design and building-height policies given full weight because there was “no substantive basis” to call them materially inconsistent with national policy: the reasoning SDC's approach to CS.8 in section 3 leaves out.",
'PINS-6006637':
 "This appeal is the closest analogue for section 5. A site only 350 m from a railway station still failed, because the route “cannot reasonably be regarded as providing a safe route for all users” and the station was not a “well-connected station” under Annex B. It also applied the 2026 Framework to an appeal already under way (section 6). It shows location is decided on the quality of the route, not on nearness to a station, and that existing use alone does not show a route is safe.",
'PINS-6010313':
 "This appeal shows a five-dwelling scheme failing the sustainable-location limb: the site was away from settlements, facilities and bus services, on a 40 mph lane with no footway or lighting, so occupiers “would be heavily reliant on private vehicles” with no genuine choice of modes. It is the route-and-services test SDC should apply, not a boundary line.",
'PINS-6011736':
 "This appeal shows the same test: a mainly unlit walk meant journeys on foot “would not be attractive to the majority of occupants”, so the location was not sustainable under TR3, even with a Connectivity Tool score the same as Poynton's.",
'PINS-6010471':
 "This appeal is the counter-example, a permission in principle found sustainably located partly because a “continuous lit footway” gave “a safe and attractive opportunity for walking”. It shows the route test cuts both ways on the facts, which is why the test that is applied, not the outcome, is the point of section 5.",
'PINS-6006900':
 "This appeal shows that a village's status does not make a site sustainable: Findon is a service village, but the site was reached along an unlit lane with no footway, and the inspector found that the development “would not be physically well related to the settlement of Findon”. It is the answer to reports that treat a village's category as evidence of a sustainable location.",
'PINS-6011231':
 "This appeal makes the same point from the other side of the balance: Banks is a Key Sustainable Village, yet the distance to its services left occupiers “largely reliant on the private car”. Because the conversion was not inappropriate in the Green Belt, that finding counted as harm in the S5(5) balance rather than as a failed limb, and the appeal was allowed.",
}
